    <div class="profile-section-block" id="foundation">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Foundation</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany.jpg" alt="Matilda of Tuscany" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Tuscany</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mathilde_und_Hugo_von_Cluny_als_F%C3%BCrsprecher_Heinrichs_IV._(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Donizo, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> began as the Roman colony of Florentia on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arno">Arno</a>, traditionally founded in 59 BCE, and in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> it was a modest town of the March of Tuscany. Its rise as a self-governing city followed the death of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/matilda-of-tuscany">Matilda of Canossa</a>, Margravine of Tuscany, in 1115. With imperial authority in Tuscany weak and disputed, the leading citizens took over the government of the city, and consuls are recorded from 1138. Like other Italian communes, Florence then set out to dominate its countryside, the <em>contado</em>: it destroyed the hill town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fiesole">Fiesole</a> in 1125, forced rural lords to live in the city for part of the year, and fought Siena, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a>, Lucca and Pistoia for roads and markets. The commune remained formally part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a>, but emperors such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-barbarossa">Frederick Barbarossa</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frederick-ii-elector-of-brandenburg">Frederick II</a> found it hard to control. By the thirteenth century the city was splitting into factions, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a>, who looked to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/capetian-house-of-anjou">Angevins</a>, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelphs-and-ghibellines">Ghibellines</a>, who supported the emperors. The popular regime of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/primo-popolo">Primo Popolo</a> (1250–1260) gave the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a> and merchants a share in power, and in 1252 the commune issued the gold florin, the coin that would carry the city’s name across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="socioeconomy">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Socioeconomy</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/florence"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence.jpg" alt="Florence" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Firenze_-_Piazzale_Michelangelo,_Firenze,_Italy_-_April_6,_2015_02.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Giorgio Galeotti, CC BY 4.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>’s wealth came from cloth and money. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arte-di-calimala">Arte di Calimala</a> finished and dyed imported cloth, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arte-della-lana">Arte della Lana</a> produced woollens from English and later Spanish wool on a large scale; the chronicler Giovanni Villani claimed some 200 workshops and about 90,000 inhabitants in the 1330s, figures historians treat as broadly plausible. Florentine merchant-bankers, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bardi-family">Bardi</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peruzzi-family">Peruzzi</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acciaiuoli-family">Acciaiuoli</a>, lent to popes and kings and collected papal revenues across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, until loans to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edward-iii-of-england">Edward III of England</a> and others helped bring them down in the 1340s. The florin, of stable weight and fineness, became a standard money of account for international trade. Society was organised by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, seven major and fourteen minor, and by neighbourhoods and lineages, with a large class of wool workers who had no guild of their own. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> of 1348 killed perhaps half the population, and in 1378 the wool workers, the <em>Ciompi</em>, briefly seized power before being suppressed. In the fifteenth century the Medici bank, founded by Giovanni di Bicci de’ Medici in 1397, became the greatest in Europe, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florentine-catasto">Catasto of 1427</a>, a tax survey of every household, is one of the richest sources for any medieval society. Wealth fed patronage: guilds, families and the commune paid for churches, palaces and public art.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="military">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Military</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montaperti"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/battle-of-montaperti.jpg" alt="Battle of Montaperti" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montaperti">Battle of Montaperti</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:How_the_Florentines_raised_an_army_to_fortify_Montalcino_and_were_discomfited_by_Count_Giordano_and_by_the_Sienese_at_Montaperti.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The early commune fought with citizen militias organised by neighbourhood, but the decisive battles of the thirteenth century were won and lost by alliances. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-montaperti">Battle of Montaperti</a> in 1260 a Ghibelline army of Siena and Florentine exiles routed the Guelph forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>, and the city was nearly destroyed; after the Angevin victory at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-benevento">Battle of Benevento</a> in 1266 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-welf">Guelphs</a> returned for good. At the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-campaldino">Battle of Campaldino</a> in 1289, where the young <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante Alighieri</a> fought among the cavalry, Florence defeated Ghibelline <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arezzo">Arezzo</a>. In the fourteenth century the republic relied increasingly on hired companies under <em>condottieri</em> such as the Englishman <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-hawkwood">John Hawkwood</a>, commemorated by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paolo-uccello">Paolo Uccello</a>’s fresco in the cathedral. Florence fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/papacy">papacy</a> in the War of the Eight Saints (1375–1378) and survived the expansion of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gian-galeazzo-visconti">Giangaleazzo Visconti</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/milan">Milan</a>, whose death in 1402 relieved the city. It took Arezzo in 1384 and, after a long siege, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pisa">Pisa</a> in 1406, gaining access to the sea, and bought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/livorno">Livorno</a> from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a> in 1421. Pisa rebelled in 1494 and was retaken only in 1509, partly by the citizen militia that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a> organised. The last republic defended itself during the siege of 1529–1530, with fortifications designed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-ferrucci">Francesco Ferrucci</a>’s relief army was destroyed at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gavinana">Gavinana</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="governance">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Governance &amp; Law</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella.jpg" alt="Giano della Bella" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella">Giano della Bella</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Giano_della_Bella.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Unknown author, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>From 1282 the government was headed by priors elected from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guild">guilds</a>, and the Ordinances of Justice of 1293, associated with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giano-della-bella">Giano della Bella</a>, excluded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magnates">magnate</a> families from the priorate and created a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gonfaloniere-of-justice">Gonfaloniere of Justice</a>. The resulting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/signoria-of-florence">Signoria</a>, from 1343 eight priors and the Gonfaloniere, sat for two months at a time, supported by advisory colleges and legislative councils, and offices were filled by drawing names from bags of approved citizens. These short terms aimed to prevent tyranny, though <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> did briefly accept lords, among them <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walter-vi-of-brienne">Walter of Brienne</a>, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a>, in 1342–1343. Factional conflict was constant: the victory of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guelphs-and-ghibellines">Black Guelphs</a> in 1301–1302 led to the exile of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>. After the Ciompi revolt an oligarchy led by the Albizzi governed from 1382. In 1434 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de’ Medici</a> returned from a year’s exile and, without holding formal power for long, controlled the republic through allies, manipulation of the electoral lists and new councils such as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/consell-de-cent">Council of One Hundred</a> and later the Council of Seventy. His grandson <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorenzo-de-medici">Lorenzo de’ Medici</a> survived the Pazzi conspiracy of 1478, in which his brother Giuliano was killed. After 1494 a Great Council on the Venetian model gave the vote to some 3,000 citizens, and in 1502 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-soderini">Piero Soderini</a> was made Gonfaloniere for life.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="impact">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Global &amp; Regional Impact</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral.jpg" alt="Florence Cathedral" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Cattedrale_di_Santa_Maria_del_Fiore_%E2%80%93_Il_Duomo_di_Firenze.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Gary Campbell-Hall, CC BY 2.0</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a>’s influence on European culture is out of proportion to its size. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dante-alighieri">Dante</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/divine-comedy">Divine Comedy</a></em>, together with the works of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/petrarch">Petrarch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giovanni-boccaccio">Giovanni Boccaccio</a>, made the Tuscan dialect of Florence the basis of literary Italian. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/giotto">Giotto di Bondone</a> transformed painting in the early fourteenth century. In the fifteenth century <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/filippo-brunelleschi">Filippo Brunelleschi</a>’s dome of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence-cathedral">Florence Cathedral</a>, completed in 1436, his use of linear perspective, the sculpture of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/donatello">Donatello</a> and the painting of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/masaccio">Masaccio</a> launched the artistic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, followed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sandro-botticelli">Sandro Botticelli</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-da-vinci">Leonardo da Vinci</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/michelangelo">Michelangelo</a>, whose <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/david">David</a></em> was set up outside the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/palazzo-vecchio">Palazzo Vecchio</a> in 1504. The chancellors <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coluccio-salutati">Coluccio Salutati</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/leonardo-bruni">Leonardo Bruni</a> shaped a civic humanism that praised republican liberty, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marsilio-ficino">Marsilio Ficino</a> translated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/plato">Plato</a> for <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cosimo-de-medici">Cosimo de’ Medici</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/council-of-florence">Council of Florence</a> of 1439 brought Byzantine scholars to the city. Florentine accounting, banking and the florin influenced commercial practice across <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. The republic’s crises also produced modern political thought: <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Niccolò Machiavelli</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-prince">The Prince</a></em> and <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/discourses-on-livy">Discourses on Livy</a></em> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/francesco-guicciardini">Francesco Guicciardini</a>’s <em><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/storia-ditalia">History of Italy</a></em> grew out of the experience of its fall. The Medici it raised gave the Church two popes, Leo X and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Clement VII</a>, and later two queens of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>.</p></div>
      </div>
      <div class="profile-section-block" id="decline">
        <h2 class="profile-section-title">Decline &amp; Fall</h2>
        <figure class="profile-section-fig"><a href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527.jpg" alt="Sack of Rome (1527)" loading="lazy" width="240"></a><figcaption><a href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome (1527)</a><span class="profile-section-fig-credit"><a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sack_of_Rome_of_1527_by_Johannes_Lingelbach_17th_century.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Johannes Lingelbach, Public domain</a></span></figcaption></figure>
        <div class="profile-section-body"><p>The republic ended in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italian-wars">Italian Wars</a>. When <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-viii-of-france">Charles VIII of France</a> invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> in 1494, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-di-lorenzo-de-medici">Piero de’ Medici</a> surrendered fortresses to him and was expelled by the citizens, and the friar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girolamo-savonarola">Girolamo Savonarola</a> dominated the restored republic until he was burned in 1498. Under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/piero-soderini">Piero Soderini</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> stayed loyal to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, and when the French withdrew from Italy in 1512 a Spanish army sacked nearby Prato and the Medici were restored. With Giovanni de’ Medici as Pope Leo X from 1513 and his cousin Giulio as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-clement-vii">Clement VII</a> from 1523, Florence was governed from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sack-of-rome-1527">Sack of Rome</a> in 1527 gave the Florentines a chance to expel the Medici once more, but the last republic was isolated after Clement VII made peace with Emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1529. Imperial and papal troops besieged the city from October 1529, and after the defeat at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-gavinana">Gavinana</a> it capitulated in August 1530. Charles V named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alessandro-de-medici">Alessandro de’ Medici</a> head of the state, and in April 1532 the old <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/signoria-of-florence">Signoria</a> was abolished and he became Duke of the Florentine Republic, a hereditary prince. Alessandro was murdered in 1537; his successor Cosimo I conquered Siena and was made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-duke-of-tuscany">Grand Duke of Tuscany</a> in 1569, ending any hope of a restored republic.</p></div>
      </div>

    <p class="profile-summary" id="summary"><em>The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florence">Florence</a> turned a Tuscan wool and banking town into the economic and cultural capital of late medieval <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>, issuing the florin, giving <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/italy">Italy</a> its literary language and launching the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/renaissance">Renaissance</a>, while its turbulent politics produced both Medici rule and the political thought of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/niccolo-machiavelli">Machiavelli</a>.</em></p>

